<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<odoo noupdate="1">

    <!--
        Canonical role identities for the ATMTA suite.

        Every group below is PERMISSION-NEUTRAL: no ACL row, no record rule, no
        menu, no model. That is the point. A capability module can depend on
        `atmta_roles` to name "Buyer" without depending on
        `real_estate_procurement`, and nobody gains a right by being given one.

        Two design choices are deliberate and were measured, not assumed.

        1. FLAT, not tiered. The architecture sketches Manager-over-User ladders.
           They are not implemented here. Odoo renders the groups of one
           `ir.module.category` as an exclusive selection when they form a
           linear implication chain, and measurement shows real users hold up to
           TEN Procurement roles at once — Requester and Buyer and Approver and
           Technical Evaluator and Manager together. A ladder would turn those
           into a single-choice dropdown and silently destroy that. Tiers can be
           added by the wave that binds ACLs, where they mean something.

        2. Every domain role implies `ATMTA User`, which implies
           `base.group_user`. That is the only implication in the file. It is
           canonical-to-canonical and canonical-to-Odoo-employee; there is no
           edge from a canonical role to a legacy business group, because such an
           edge would hand over that group's ACLs, rules and menus the moment
           anyone was given the role.
    -->

    <record id="module_category_atmta_development" model="ir.module.category">
        <field name="name">Development</field>
        <field name="parent_id" ref="atmta_base.module_category_atmta"/>
        <field name="sequence">10</field>
    </record>

